WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.530
- We're doing the book
tour and all of a sudden,

00:00:01.530 --> 00:00:03.756
I get this email, that the Huffington Post

00:00:03.756 --> 00:00:07.020
is saying that George W. Bush
plagiarized some of his book.

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:09.231
I'm like, "What's he talking about?"

00:00:09.231 --> 00:00:13.440
Because, I knew that he
and a former speech writer

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.616
had worked on all of it together.

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:16.490
Like, "What are they talking about?"

00:00:16.490 --> 00:00:17.680
So I read it.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.730
I'm like, "Oh my gosh."

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.740
So this reporter for Huffington Post.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:24.310
Wrote that in Bob Woodward's book

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:26.710
from like three years ago,

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:28.580
there's a passage in there in which

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.553
Bob Woodward quotes somebody,

00:00:31.553 --> 00:00:34.793
who was a source close to the President,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:38.463
who said this about the Iraq War.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.733
Therefore, they said that that

00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.540
(laughing)
that George W. Bush,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.060
who had then written that
- I got it.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.700
- In Decision Points, that therefore,

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:47.773
he was plagiarizing Bob Woodward.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.977
I was like, "Obviously, he's the source

00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:55.020
"close to the President, is
the President, you dumbass."

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:56.398
Sorry, can we say that on here?

00:00:56.398 --> 00:00:58.981
(upbeat music)

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:07.560
- Hey, I'm Dave Rubin and
this is the Rubin Report.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.450
Reminder everybody.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:12.460
Get all our videos and audio
podcasts, absolutely ad free

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.800
and early at RubinReport.com.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.770
More importantly, joining me today,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.080
is the host of the Daily Briefing
on Fox News, Dana Perino,

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.772
finally, welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:01:23.772 --> 00:01:25.648
- I'm so glad to be here,
we've been wanting to

00:01:25.648 --> 00:01:28.130
do this for a long time.

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:28.963
- We have been wanting to do it.

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:30.070
We wanted to do it in person,

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:32.350
which is why we never did it via Skype.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.300
But these freaking pandemics.

00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:36.718
Is there anything worse than a pandemic?

00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:40.003
- I mean it really did get in
the way of a lot of things.

00:01:40.003 --> 00:01:42.049
- (laughs) It really did, so clearly,

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:44.180
you're not in studio at the moment.

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:45.700
- No.
- You are at home

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:47.410
and you've been kind of sharing,

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:49.010
you're trying to, I think do something

00:01:49.010 --> 00:01:50.570
similar to what I've been trying to do.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:53.360
Just kind of like share
your life a little bit more.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:55.770
Your dog, what you're
eating, what you're cooking,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.723
all of that stuff, in
the midst of this thing.

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:02.293
How is life, in the midst of a pandemic?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.483
- Well life is good, right?

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:10.060
That we're here and that we're busy

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:11.460
and that we're working.

00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:12.950
Life for me, I have to say,

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:16.483
I just don't have complaints,
or room to complain.

00:02:18.107 --> 00:02:23.000
I try to live my life
with a lot of gratitude.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.730
Then you go through something like this,

00:02:24.730 --> 00:02:27.870
where you see what other
people are going through.

00:02:27.870 --> 00:02:32.050
Sometimes I find myself
kind of holding my breath

00:02:32.050 --> 00:02:33.725
and I realize that it's because

00:02:33.725 --> 00:02:36.680
I'm really worried about,

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:38.670
well people's health, number one.

00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.940
But I also am worried
that all of these people,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.990
who are now having to ask
for government assistance,

00:02:44.990 --> 00:02:47.020
where they never would have done that,

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.020
in their life, like they'd rather die

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.030
than ask the government for a dime.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:54.510
Yet here, the government has had to

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.900
force people to not work.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:02:59.840
And force them out of jobs
and now they're in a position

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:02.500
of having to apply for unemployment.

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.170
The government is trying to be generous,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.640
but it's also the government and it's hard

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:08.540
and it's lumbering and it's just difficult

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:11.980
and so, whereas things
for us, are pretty good

00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:16.940
and actually, I kind of
needed this forced pause,

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.600
because I was so over scheduled,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:21.560
and trying to please too many people

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.130
and doing too many events
and dinners and things.

00:03:24.130 --> 00:03:26.350
So the fact that I've
had time to reconnect

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.500
with my husband and I've
cooked more in three weeks

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.930
than in probably
(laughing)

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.672
22 years, because,
- And I like

00:03:34.672 --> 00:03:35.570
- We're in the city
- that you put

00:03:35.570 --> 00:03:36.554
- And it's easy to--
- the pictures.

00:03:36.554 --> 00:03:39.240
You put pictures even
admitting some of these things

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:40.860
are experiments and they seem to

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.360
be working.
- Yeah (laughs).

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.782
Yeah I made, I got, so I have a Peloton.

00:03:45.782 --> 00:03:49.550
One of my favorite instructors
is Hannah Marie Corbin.

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:51.710
I read an article where she talked about

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:53.610
what she eats in a day.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:57.490
She recommended this cookbook
called, "Oh She Glows."

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.930
It's a vegan cookbook and I'm not a vegan.

00:03:59.930 --> 00:04:02.530
Grew up on a cattle
ranch, so I'm not a vegan.

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.930
But, I know I need to eat more vegetables.

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:07.790
So I've been making some
recipes out of that book

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.996
and Peter likes them.

00:04:09.996 --> 00:04:13.740
I like them, so I made
sweet potato enchiladas,

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.593
that felt like a big accomplishment.

00:04:17.240 --> 00:04:19.870
- But Dana, have you taken
my advice that I gave you

00:04:19.870 --> 00:04:22.213
on your show last week and
started growing Romaine

00:04:22.213 --> 00:04:23.460
out of the butts
(laughing)

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:24.750
of other Romaine?

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:25.777
Have you done that yet?

00:04:25.777 --> 00:04:28.133
- I have thought about that so many times.

00:04:29.003 --> 00:04:30.410
(laughing)
I am going to try,

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:32.240
the butts of the Romaine.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.193
The thing is, okay so my
day starts before six a.m.

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:39.280
It ends at nine p.m.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.910
So, I've literally been
house bound for three weeks

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.730
and my husband, who
normally works from home,

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.938
he's done any of the
errands that we've had,

00:04:47.938 --> 00:04:51.210
mostly going to the
grocery store or picking up

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.160
from local restaurants that we support.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:58.190
So I haven't been able
to get the Romaine butts,

00:04:58.190 --> 00:04:59.363
but I'm going to.

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:01.430
- But you're going to.

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:02.263
All right, fair enough.

00:05:02.263 --> 00:05:03.660
So I like that you said
this thing about that

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:06.960
you needed sort of this unforced break.

00:05:06.960 --> 00:05:09.630
Do you think a lot of people
are suddenly feeling that?

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:11.480
Like, just the way life is

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:13.420
and how fast everything seems to move

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:16.270
and social media and
the 24 hour news cycle

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:17.450
that you're well aware of,

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.810
that we suddenly got
this break and of course,

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:20.910
it's not for a good reason,

00:05:20.910 --> 00:05:24.250
but that basically, if
you're okay right now

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:25.734
and you've got a roof over your head

00:05:25.734 --> 00:05:27.093
and hopefully you have a couple bucks,

00:05:27.093 --> 00:05:29.501
that there really is an
incredible opportunity

00:05:29.501 --> 00:05:30.975
to kind of look at your life

00:05:30.975 --> 00:05:33.681
and look at the world a
little bit differently.

00:05:33.681 --> 00:05:35.310
- Absolutely.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:37.403
If you read any,

00:05:39.470 --> 00:05:41.187
anyone saying like, "Well what do you

00:05:41.187 --> 00:05:42.610
"really want in your life?"

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.970
It's that people feel over busy.

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:47.037
I made a New Year's resolution
that when people said,

00:05:47.037 --> 00:05:48.120
"How are you doing?"

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.350
This was a couple years ago.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.870
That I would stop saying, "I'm so busy."

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:54.440
Because, everybody is so busy.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.920
Everybody is trying
and striving and doing.

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.327
Nobody says, "Oh I'm
fine, totally kicked back.

00:05:59.327 --> 00:06:02.230
"I get eight hours of sleep
a night, everything's good."

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:03.490
Everybody has stuff.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:05.680
So I stopped saying that
a couple of years ago

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:08.773
and that was a good sort
of mental shift for me.

00:06:10.260 --> 00:06:11.773
But I know it in myself.

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:15.620
I have a very hard time
saying no to opportunities

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:16.960
that come my way.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.140
Everything is amazing.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.377
Especially like in New York City.

00:06:20.377 --> 00:06:22.687
"Would you speak at this event

00:06:22.687 --> 00:06:24.077
"for this wonderful charity?

00:06:24.077 --> 00:06:26.048
"What about for this veteran's group?"

00:06:26.048 --> 00:06:29.552
I can be out week nights,

00:06:29.552 --> 00:06:33.060
every single night of the week

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:35.660
and then trying to catch
up on life on the weekends.

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:38.170
What I've realized is that,

00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.170
having to tap the breaks because,

00:06:40.170 --> 00:06:43.034
we have to all do our
part in this pandemic,

00:06:43.034 --> 00:06:45.520
it's been actually really good for me

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:47.623
and I don't have,

00:06:48.890 --> 00:06:50.690
I know that I can't hold
on to this completely,

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:52.674
when all of us return to normal.

00:06:52.674 --> 00:06:55.540
But I would like to figure
out a way that I could

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.080
hold on to a little bit of it,

00:06:57.080 --> 00:07:00.780
because, it is better for me.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:01.810
A lot better.

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.210
- Yeah, well it's interesting,

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:05.420
it's one of the reasons
why I like doing your show,

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:08.130
because, even though you're
doing informational stuff,

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:11.140
you're doing like the daily
grind political stuff.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:13.010
There is this other feeling
that I think you have,

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.830
that a lot of shows kind of miss,

00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.060
which is that there's something
that's going on in the world

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:20.320
at all times, that doesn't have
to do with politics exactly.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.700
I think for you guys,

00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:22.690
one of the cool things for me,

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:23.523
over the last couple years,

00:07:23.523 --> 00:07:24.780
is I've gotten to know, I would say,

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:27.410
you, Greg Gutfeld, who's
a good buddy of yours

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.340
and Tucker Carlson specifically,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:30.870
it's like when we watch you guys,

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:32.190
there's something a little bit different

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:34.510
about watching someone on cable news,

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:35.550
than on the internet.

00:07:35.550 --> 00:07:38.630
Like it's almost like, "Are
these people actual humans?"

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:40.660
I think you've been able
to now show that window,

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:41.810
like, "Oh, there's a human here.

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:42.967
"Here is the dog.

00:07:42.967 --> 00:07:44.210
"We're real people."

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:45.170
That actually

00:07:45.170 --> 00:07:47.110
- You know--
- has been nice during this.

00:07:47.110 --> 00:07:47.943
- You know it's interesting,

00:07:47.943 --> 00:07:50.670
I give Greg Gutfeld a ton of credit for

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:52.480
actually bringing me along on that,

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:56.710
because, we started The
Five, in July of 2011.

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.418
Before that, I'd been the press secretary

00:07:58.418 --> 00:08:01.077
and you know, that's a
pretty serious job (laughs).

00:08:01.947 --> 00:08:02.780
(laughing)
Even though

00:08:02.780 --> 00:08:04.003
I was in the public eye,

00:08:05.260 --> 00:08:07.970
I didn't do a ton of personal things,

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:08.803
like I would never,

00:08:08.803 --> 00:08:10.530
well we didn't have
Instagram or Twitter then.

00:08:10.530 --> 00:08:13.400
So, I didn't really share my life,

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:15.030
'cause I thought, "Who would care?"

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.337
Well then we start doing The
Five and we would be asked,

00:08:18.337 --> 00:08:20.244
"What do you think about this?"

00:08:20.244 --> 00:08:24.440
Almost always, I was
instinctively giving the answer

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.260
as if I was still the
White House press secretary

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.089
for George W. Bush.

00:08:28.089 --> 00:08:31.767
At one point, Greg said,
"No, what do you think?"

00:08:32.620 --> 00:08:35.550
It was about drug legalization,
marijuana legalization.

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.230
I've never smoked pot.

00:08:37.230 --> 00:08:38.730
I'm not into it.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.450
I have a very unpopular opinion.

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:41.420
I don't think it's a good idea.

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:44.620
But I realized, I just froze.

00:08:44.620 --> 00:08:47.500
I had never given my personal opinion

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:50.747
about anything out loud.

00:08:50.747 --> 00:08:53.500
He pushed me a little
and teased me a little

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:57.423
and made it okay for me to,

00:08:57.423 --> 00:08:58.830
it was kind of just more be myself

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:02.133
and not have to be the press
secretary, so much anymore.

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:05.987
So I get asked in an interview sometimes,

00:09:05.987 --> 00:09:08.577
"Is there something that
people don't know about you?"

00:09:08.577 --> 00:09:10.500
I'm like, "Nope."

00:09:10.500 --> 00:09:13.290
Like it's been 10 years,
we've been doing The Five

00:09:13.290 --> 00:09:14.326
and I was on Red Eye
- (laughs) Yeah.

00:09:14.326 --> 00:09:15.159
- Before all that.

00:09:15.159 --> 00:09:17.310
There is pretty much nothing that

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:18.320
people don't know about me.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.600
So Greg actually really helped with that.

00:09:20.600 --> 00:09:22.770
Then especially on The Five,

00:09:22.770 --> 00:09:25.280
we take the news seriously,

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.060
but we don't take
ourselves that seriously.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.060
Life can be sweet and God
meant for us to be joyous.

00:09:34.390 --> 00:09:37.860
I really learned that from
President George Bush too.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:41.050
We worked very hard, but we were always

00:09:41.050 --> 00:09:43.950
celebrating the joys of
getting the chance to serve

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.470
at the highest levels of government,

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.620
for the best country
the world's ever seen.

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.810
- Yeah, so all right, let's
back up to that for a second.

00:09:50.810 --> 00:09:52.580
So for the people that don't
know, you just referenced,

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.140
but you were press secretary
for George W. Bush.

00:09:55.140 --> 00:09:56.270
That means you were the person.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:57.486
I remember watching you,

00:09:57.486 --> 00:09:59.258
(laughing)
back in the day, you know?

00:09:59.258 --> 00:10:00.352
- Really short hair.
- You were up there,

00:10:00.352 --> 00:10:02.566
(laughs) yeah and you were answering,

00:10:02.566 --> 00:10:05.060
you were answering, or at least
to the best of the ability

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.240
of a press secretary, answering
a lot of tough questions

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.130
related to foreign policy and the economy

00:10:09.130 --> 00:10:11.050
and all kinds of stuff.

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:14.810
Can you give me like some
insight into what that job

00:10:14.810 --> 00:10:17.620
was like then, versus maybe how it is now.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:19.955
I mean Trump barely has
to use a press secretary,

00:10:19.955 --> 00:10:22.210
'cause he's just kind of out there.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:23.140
Then the networks now,

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:25.480
CNN doesn't even cover
those things anymore.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.730
You guys cover it, but how that sort of

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.090
has changed over, you know,

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:31.230
it's been about what, 20
years or so, 15 years.

00:10:31.230 --> 00:10:36.010
- What it's interesting,
in January of 2009.

00:10:36.010 --> 00:10:39.890
So January 20, 2009, the
Bushes turned things over

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.440
to the Obamas.

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.550
I was there for the last day

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:45.600
and then Peter and I left for Africa.

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:47.860
We went to South Africa
for a two weeks vacation

00:10:47.860 --> 00:10:50.513
and two weeks volunteering
at a PEPFAR site.

00:10:51.759 --> 00:10:55.740
I really wanted that time
to reset my priorities,

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:58.933
reset my marriage, reset my brain.

00:10:58.933 --> 00:11:00.740
To get ready for this transition.

00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:03.310
But I think back on that
and I didn't even have

00:11:03.310 --> 00:11:04.960
a Twitter account, at that point.

00:11:06.389 --> 00:11:08.200
If you think just, that was 2009.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.770
Think in 10 years how things
changed so dramatically.

00:11:11.770 --> 00:11:14.110
And you think about the Obama campaign,

00:11:14.110 --> 00:11:17.850
that absolutely smoked the
Republicans on social media

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.930
in 2008 campaign and then,

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:24.840
in 2016, President Trump realizes that

00:11:24.840 --> 00:11:27.540
he can talk directly to people

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.720
and he was very comfortable with that.

00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.580
I don't know if George Bush ever was.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.030
Sometimes I laugh and think about

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:38.740
writing a book of Tweets
Bush would've sent (laughs),

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:41.053
which, probably would've been
(laughing)

00:11:41.053 --> 00:11:43.440
would've been pretty funny.
- It's actually a great idea.

00:11:43.440 --> 00:11:46.500
- But it has changed so much.

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:48.070
Like now I will say this.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:52.793
I think for the country, briefings
from the press secretary,

00:11:53.700 --> 00:11:56.040
I always tried to turn down
the temperature in the room.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.027
I thought, "Why are we fighting?

00:11:57.027 --> 00:11:58.567
"I'm here to give information,

00:11:58.567 --> 00:12:00.580
"you're here to illicit information."

00:12:00.580 --> 00:12:02.500
I always remembered too,
that those reporters

00:12:02.500 --> 00:12:04.260
have a job to do.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.870
Part of my job as press
secretary I believed was,

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:10.200
protecting their right to
have access to the government.

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:12.120
But here's the other thing.

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.630
I truly believe that a press briefing,

00:12:14.630 --> 00:12:16.440
a daily press briefing,

00:12:16.440 --> 00:12:20.120
forces a White House to
have really good process.

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:22.660
Because if they know that
the press secretary's

00:12:22.660 --> 00:12:24.940
gonna have to go out there
and she's gonna have to

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.450
answer these questions and that means

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.130
you better call her back first,

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:31.740
if there's problems and
the policy isn't settled,

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:33.830
everybody needs to get on
the phone and get it done.

00:12:33.830 --> 00:12:35.780
Another thing I used to do was,

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.390
let's say I was, we were at
war when I was press secretary.

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.710
So things are all so different.

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:43.380
But let's say something was happening

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:45.310
and it was a national security issue

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.390
and somebody within the government

00:12:47.390 --> 00:12:51.680
suggested I say, such and
such answer at the briefing.

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:55.840
Well, what I did, because I
was a reporter by training is,

00:12:55.840 --> 00:12:58.010
I would double check and triple check.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.577
So I had my deputies and I would say,

00:12:59.577 --> 00:13:02.097
"Gordon, can you call the
National Security Council,

00:13:02.097 --> 00:13:04.337
"see if this is what they say.

00:13:04.337 --> 00:13:06.343
"Tony's gonna call DOD.

00:13:07.217 --> 00:13:09.217
"You call the CIA.

00:13:09.217 --> 00:13:11.227
"Let's meet back here in 20 minutes

00:13:11.227 --> 00:13:13.637
"and if everybody has the same answer,

00:13:13.637 --> 00:13:15.400
"I'll say it at the podium."

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.420
And if we don't, then I would go to the

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:18.647
Chief of Staff's office and I'd say,

00:13:18.647 --> 00:13:22.307
"We've got a problem here,
because everybody's not settled

00:13:22.307 --> 00:13:25.137
"and we have to get this
right, because people's lives

00:13:25.137 --> 00:13:26.590
"are on the line."

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:29.800
So, in that case, I feel
like forcing good process

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:34.176
is really good, but,
everything's changed so much.

00:13:34.176 --> 00:13:36.960
Who knows if it'll ever go back to being

00:13:36.960 --> 00:13:37.793
a daily press briefing.

00:13:37.793 --> 00:13:39.943
No, the President, oh he just finished,

00:13:39.943 --> 00:13:41.494
you know he--
- Well he does them.

00:13:41.494 --> 00:13:42.610
- (laughs) He does them now.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:45.601
I think he's starting to enjoy
it, in the briefing room.

00:13:45.601 --> 00:13:48.120
- Well he's having a hell
of a time in there, clearly.

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.440
I mean from the little bit that I can,

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:52.430
I don't have time either
to watch these huge things

00:13:52.430 --> 00:13:53.280
in their,
- I know.

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.430
- In their totality,
but when I watch them,

00:13:55.430 --> 00:13:57.370
I'm like, "Yeah, he's
actually having fun."

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:59.700
But that sort of gets to what
I wanted to ask you about

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:02.920
just sort of the general way
we talk about presidents.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.170
So it's like I remember
during the Iraq War

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:06.810
and you being up there and all the things,

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:09.400
I lived on the Upper
West of New York City,

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:11.850
the most liberal place on Earth.

00:14:11.850 --> 00:14:14.970
Outside Zabar's they'd
have the book tables

00:14:14.970 --> 00:14:17.700
and every book was George
W. Bush is the devil

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:19.290
and evil and all of that stuff.

00:14:19.290 --> 00:14:22.000
Where now, the media
because he's not Trump,

00:14:22.000 --> 00:14:23.930
they sort of treat him like a nice guy.

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:24.960
He's also a little older.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.923
He paints now, so he's kind of

00:14:26.923 --> 00:14:28.490
(laughing)
good now and Trump

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:30.080
is the ultimate evil.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:32.770
Do you see that sort of
always churning through

00:14:32.770 --> 00:14:33.950
like every 10 years,

00:14:33.950 --> 00:14:35.770
where the media takes someone they hate,

00:14:35.770 --> 00:14:37.990
now they're good, 'cause
they have a new bad guy,

00:14:37.990 --> 00:14:41.340
and that these pieces will
always sort of shift around?

00:14:41.340 --> 00:14:43.770
- I have a great story for you about that.

00:14:43.770 --> 00:14:47.330
Marlin Fitzwater, was the press
secretary for Ronald Regan

00:14:47.330 --> 00:14:49.010
and George H.W. Bush.

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.610
So for eight years.

00:14:50.610 --> 00:14:52.710
He was the press
secretary for eight years.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:53.816
I joke with him that

00:14:53.816 --> 00:14:54.649
- That's a long time.
- He could never have

00:14:54.649 --> 00:14:56.763
done that in the age of social media, but.

00:14:58.114 --> 00:15:02.700
He told me that, well actually
I invited him to my house

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.290
in October of 2008.

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:08.500
I invited all of my staff
from the press room.

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:10.090
I made them all read his book,

00:15:10.090 --> 00:15:12.980
one of the best books ever
written in Washington D.C.,

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:15.920
as a memoir, it's called,
"Call the Briefing."

00:15:15.920 --> 00:15:16.880
By Marlin Fitzwater.

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:20.290
It's fantastic, his
storytelling is spectacular.

00:15:20.290 --> 00:15:23.090
So he came and we had a little book club.

00:15:23.090 --> 00:15:26.240
And, he delivered a message to all of us

00:15:26.240 --> 00:15:29.257
and he said, "I know you're feeling like

00:15:29.257 --> 00:15:32.900
"your boss is really being
dragged through the mud."

00:15:32.900 --> 00:15:34.900
Our approval ratings were terrible.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.410
This is right after the financial crisis,

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:37.383
as you can imagine.

00:15:38.300 --> 00:15:41.437
He said, "Let me tell you
a story about in 1992."

00:15:42.391 --> 00:15:45.780
In January of '92, they had lost, or '93.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.280
They had lost to Bill Clinton.

00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:52.670
He said it was so mortifying
to lose to Bill Clinton

00:15:52.670 --> 00:15:55.928
and he said, "When we left that day."

00:15:55.928 --> 00:15:59.487
He said, "I had my head
was hanging so low,

00:15:59.487 --> 00:16:02.837
"because I thought no one
will ever, ever understand

00:16:02.837 --> 00:16:06.587
"and see what a great man
George H.W. Bush was."

00:16:07.640 --> 00:16:11.410
Well, that changed.

00:16:11.410 --> 00:16:15.360
You know time has a way
of changing perspective

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:18.060
and 43 used to say, when I
would prep him for interviews

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:20.117
toward the end, I said,
"Sir, they're gonna ask you

00:16:20.117 --> 00:16:22.540
"what you think your
legacy is going to be."

00:16:22.540 --> 00:16:25.987
He said, "Look, you know,
last year I read three books

00:16:25.987 --> 00:16:27.807
"about George Washington.

00:16:27.807 --> 00:16:31.677
"And if historians are still
analyzing the first president,

00:16:31.677 --> 00:16:33.663
"then the 43rd doesn't
have a lot to worry about

00:16:33.663 --> 00:16:35.870
(laughing)
"'cause he'll never know."

00:16:35.870 --> 00:16:38.450
And he would point out
that Abraham Lincoln died

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.000
thinking that the country
thought he was a failure.

00:16:42.130 --> 00:16:44.980
So, yes I think that,

00:16:44.980 --> 00:16:49.440
the fact that 43 could
help me lift my gaze,

00:16:49.440 --> 00:16:52.140
from current crisis and current feelings

00:16:52.140 --> 00:16:54.873
and lift my gaze and understand
there's a longer view,

00:16:55.880 --> 00:16:58.280
that really helped me
in my life, actually,

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:00.283
over all, not just in politics.

00:17:01.280 --> 00:17:02.430
- Yeah, I love that.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.510
Can you talk a little
bit about just generally,

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:07.020
what it's like, sort of working at Fox

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.100
and being in the eye of the storm,

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:12.450
where often, you guys are as critiqued

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:16.040
as the politicians you're
supposed to be talking about?

00:17:16.040 --> 00:17:18.880
This is a particularly
unique thing to Fox,

00:17:18.880 --> 00:17:22.100
where every given day, we
know that Media Matters

00:17:22.100 --> 00:17:23.460
is watching you guys,

00:17:23.460 --> 00:17:26.790
waiting for you to stumble over some word.

00:17:26.790 --> 00:17:28.220
Then they clip that video.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:30.850
They get it out to Huff
Po, they get it to VOX.

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.020
They try to, you know,
they lie about timelines.

00:17:33.020 --> 00:17:34.810
Greg's dealing with a
version of this right now,

00:17:34.810 --> 00:17:37.220
related to Coronavirus.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:41.010
How in a weird way, the
stories then become about you,

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:44.070
or that somebody's boycotting
Tucker on any given day.

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:45.793
Just that whole thing.

00:17:46.970 --> 00:17:49.580
- So I have a ton of respect for

00:17:49.580 --> 00:17:52.926
the media relations department
at Fox News because,

00:17:52.926 --> 00:17:54.700
(laughing)
they are dealing with,

00:17:54.700 --> 00:17:58.040
they are drinking from a
fire hose, every single day.

00:17:58.040 --> 00:18:01.010
And we have strong
personalities, at the network

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:03.260
and we have the freedom to
say what we want to say.

00:18:03.260 --> 00:18:06.800
I'm usually pretty,
pretty much left alone.

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:08.170
I guess maybe I don't say anything

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.770
that controversial or interesting,

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:11.930
(laughs) for them to bother.

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:15.691
But I don't like it when
someone who is expressing

00:18:15.691 --> 00:18:19.260
an opinion and then it gets distorted,

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:22.110
I really can't stand that
and this is an example

00:18:22.110 --> 00:18:23.880
that I wanted to mention
that just came to mind.

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.660
It doesn't have exactly to do with Fox,

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:28.000
but it's a good example of my experience

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.200
of dealing with the media

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:31.143
and you mentioned the Huffington Post.

00:18:33.281 --> 00:18:36.517
So, I did the PR for
George W. Bush's memoir,

00:18:36.517 --> 00:18:39.203
"Decision Points", when
it came out in 2010.

00:18:40.920 --> 00:18:43.432
We're out there, the book is launched.

00:18:43.432 --> 00:18:46.420
Going pretty well, although 43, boy,

00:18:46.420 --> 00:18:48.140
he had not done interviews in two years

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:50.610
and he was not excited
to see the press again,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.131
I'll tell you that.

00:18:52.131 --> 00:18:53.036
- (laughs) I bet.

00:18:53.036 --> 00:18:53.869
(laughing)
Well he really,

00:18:53.869 --> 00:18:55.730
you know, often presidents
disappear for a little while,

00:18:55.730 --> 00:18:58.380
but he sort of, he really
just went under the radar.

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:00.240
Finally, there was that
picture of him painting

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:01.124
and people were like,
- He said that,

00:19:01.124 --> 00:19:01.957
- "Oh what happened?"

00:19:01.957 --> 00:19:03.957
- Yeah, he said, for a long time he said,

00:19:03.957 --> 00:19:05.357
"I don't seek the (mumbles) lights.

00:19:05.357 --> 00:19:07.533
"I don't want this attention."

00:19:07.533 --> 00:19:10.340
He knew that there was another chapter,

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.400
and that he had watched his dad

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.250
be such a gracious former president

00:19:14.250 --> 00:19:17.080
and that was his role model.

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:18.100
But back to the book tour.

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.880
So, we're doing the book
tour and all of a sudden,

00:19:19.880 --> 00:19:23.010
I get this email that the
Huffington Post is saying that,

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.210
George W. Bush plagiarized
some of his book.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:27.537
Like, "What is he talking about?"

00:19:27.537 --> 00:19:31.810
Because, I knew that he
and a former speech writer

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:33.822
had worked on all of it together.

00:19:33.822 --> 00:19:34.962
Like, "What are they talking about?"

00:19:34.962 --> 00:19:36.060
So I read it.

00:19:36.060 --> 00:19:37.090
I'm like, "Oh my gosh."

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:40.100
So this reporter for Huffington Post

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:42.660
wrote that in Bob Woodward's book,

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:45.080
from like three years ago.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:47.870
There's a passage in there
in which Bob Woodward,

00:19:47.870 --> 00:19:52.870
quotes somebody, who was a
source close to the President,

00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:56.833
who said this about the Iraq War.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:20:00.060
Therefore, they said that that

00:20:00.060 --> 00:20:00.900
(laughing)
that George W. Bush

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:02.420
who had then written that
- I got it.

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:04.060
- In Decision Points, that therefore,

00:20:04.060 --> 00:20:06.163
he was plagiarizing Bob Woodward.

00:20:07.069 --> 00:20:10.337
I was like, "Obviously, he's the source

00:20:10.337 --> 00:20:13.420
"close to the President, is
the President, you dumbass."

00:20:13.420 --> 00:20:15.750
Sorry, can we say that on here?

00:20:15.750 --> 00:20:16.583
- Hey, you can say
- Okay (laughs).

00:20:16.583 --> 00:20:18.166
- Anything here, come on now

00:20:18.166 --> 00:20:20.193
(laughs).
- I remember how mad I was.

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:23.680
It just so happened that
the publisher of the book

00:20:23.680 --> 00:20:27.170
was also the publisher of
Arianna Huffington's books.

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:30.210
So, I was getting nowhere with
the reporter to take it down.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:34.130
So she said that she would
call and get her to handle it.

00:20:34.130 --> 00:20:36.693
Do you know, they would not take it down?

00:20:38.580 --> 00:20:42.520
And, so I'm not a
stranger to this behavior

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.060
in how they treated him
or other conservatives,

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:47.313
or Trump now today.

00:20:48.480 --> 00:20:51.430
It's just unfortunate, but you
just have to work around it.

00:20:52.630 --> 00:20:55.060
- Yeah, but it must take a certain amount

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:57.790
of your mental stamina during the day,

00:20:57.790 --> 00:20:59.519
right, knowing that these guys,

00:20:59.519 --> 00:21:01.650
or even when you're just
talking on your show

00:21:01.650 --> 00:21:03.521
and you might accidentally say ass

00:21:03.521 --> 00:21:05.683
and then you go, "Uh-oh,

00:21:05.683 --> 00:21:06.516
"now what have I done?"
- Oh yeah like that time

00:21:06.516 --> 00:21:08.870
that I said that the guy
carrying the alligator

00:21:08.870 --> 00:21:11.240
with his mouth taped in
the convenience store.

00:21:11.240 --> 00:21:12.073
It was during one more thing

00:21:12.073 --> 00:21:14.178
and I said, "What an asshole."

00:21:14.178 --> 00:21:15.390
(laughing)
And oh my gosh,

00:21:15.390 --> 00:21:16.703
and that's unusual for me.

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:19.660
And it caused such an uproar,

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:21.210
but we turned it into something funny

00:21:21.210 --> 00:21:23.150
and actually, the president of the company

00:21:23.150 --> 00:21:25.227
got a call from his mom and she said,

00:21:25.227 --> 00:21:27.317
"Please don't get mad at Dana.

00:21:27.317 --> 00:21:29.157
"Don't get her in any trouble.

00:21:29.157 --> 00:21:32.397
"She didn't mean to say
it, it was an accident

00:21:32.397 --> 00:21:35.708
"and besides, she was right,
the guy was an asshole."

00:21:35.708 --> 00:21:36.541
(laughing)

00:21:36.541 --> 00:21:37.760
- He was, there you go.

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:39.002
- Florida, man.
- Can you talk

00:21:39.002 --> 00:21:42.560
a little bit about the
general culture at Fox?

00:21:42.560 --> 00:21:44.280
Because you know, until I started going

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:46.830
on a bunch of these
shows, like a year ago.

00:21:46.830 --> 00:21:48.470
I don't know, I probably thought,

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:50.770
whatever maybe the
mainstream zeitgeist is.

00:21:50.770 --> 00:21:52.900
But I know that, you know,
I've been doing your show

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:54.670
basically once a week for awhile now

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:56.210
and I go on Greg and Tucker.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:21:57.730
Nobody asks me anything.

00:21:57.730 --> 00:21:59.390
You know the first few
times I went on Tucker,

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:00.927
they would say, "Oh do you have some ideas

00:22:00.927 --> 00:22:02.260
"that you wanna talk about?"

00:22:02.260 --> 00:22:03.500
But now you guys invite me.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:05.130
I go on, we don't discuss anything.

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:06.862
I know that I could say anything

00:22:06.862 --> 00:22:10.240
in those few minutes and
you may not agree with me,

00:22:10.240 --> 00:22:12.170
but you'd be okay with
it and we can joke around

00:22:12.170 --> 00:22:13.100
and the rest of it.

00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:14.277
People will always say to me,

00:22:14.277 --> 00:22:17.003
"Well Dave, why don't
you go on CNN and MSNBC?"

00:22:17.003 --> 00:22:18.967
I'm like, "Well they don't invite me.

00:22:18.967 --> 00:22:20.250
"If they did,
- What's the point?

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.118
- "As long as?"
- Oh they don't, yeah.

00:22:22.118 --> 00:22:24.670
- Well I guess what's the
point, also, nobody's watching.

00:22:24.670 --> 00:22:27.260
But you know, they don't invite me.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:28.093
I can't just show up.

00:22:28.093 --> 00:22:28.926
- Or,
- I actually have

00:22:28.926 --> 00:22:30.320
showed up at--
- What's the point

00:22:30.320 --> 00:22:32.360
if you think you're
not going to be allowed

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:34.340
to just be yourself and express your views

00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:35.563
and have that respected?

00:22:36.850 --> 00:22:38.437
- Yeah, but the meme would be,

00:22:38.437 --> 00:22:40.177
"Oh Fox News is just pushing out

00:22:40.177 --> 00:22:41.570
"talking points to everybody."

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:42.880
But I just know it's not true,

00:22:42.880 --> 00:22:45.500
because you guys let
me say whatever I want.

00:22:45.500 --> 00:22:49.580
- Yeah, I've been there,
since April of '09.

00:22:49.580 --> 00:22:52.640
I've never once been
asked to say something,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:54.920
press a message, anything.

00:22:54.920 --> 00:22:56.670
This has never happened to me,

00:22:56.670 --> 00:22:57.970
or to anybody that I know.

00:22:58.920 --> 00:23:00.510
I think one of the best things about Fox,

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:02.990
is it's a culture of respecting everybody

00:23:02.990 --> 00:23:07.013
as an individual, but working
as a very close knit team.

00:23:08.370 --> 00:23:10.763
One thing that people might not know,

00:23:11.910 --> 00:23:16.910
is that, Sean Hannity
has been the Papa Bear

00:23:17.340 --> 00:23:20.223
of so many careers at Fox, including mine.

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:23.840
When I first left the White House,

00:23:23.840 --> 00:23:25.530
you know, he knew that I was interested

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:27.520
in doing a contributor work,

00:23:27.520 --> 00:23:29.840
or at least continuing to have,

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:32.393
continuing to be a part
of the conversation.

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:34.710
So he recommended me

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:36.477
and apparently, Roger
Ailes said something like,

00:23:36.477 --> 00:23:38.887
"Well why do we need
another Bush Republican,

00:23:38.887 --> 00:23:40.537
"we already have Karl Rove."

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:45.217
Sean said, "She's different, trust me,

00:23:45.217 --> 00:23:47.200
"let me give her a shot."

00:23:47.200 --> 00:23:49.570
And he started inviting
me on his show once a week

00:23:49.570 --> 00:23:52.380
and eventually, that's
what led me to my contract

00:23:52.380 --> 00:23:55.040
with Fox News and every step of the way,

00:23:55.040 --> 00:23:58.163
this man has been so encouraging of me.

00:23:59.010 --> 00:24:00.690
Every step of the way.

00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:02.590
When The Five started,
he could not have been

00:24:02.590 --> 00:24:03.830
more excited for us.

00:24:03.830 --> 00:24:05.570
When A Daily Briefing started,

00:24:05.570 --> 00:24:07.620
he also is so excited and when I see him

00:24:07.620 --> 00:24:10.270
on occasion in the hallways now,

00:24:10.270 --> 00:24:13.107
he'll say, "I can't
hear what you're saying,

00:24:13.107 --> 00:24:16.017
"because your show is
on during my radio show,

00:24:16.017 --> 00:24:17.937
"but I have the closed caption on

00:24:17.937 --> 00:24:20.117
"and you look amazing
and your guests are great

00:24:20.117 --> 00:24:21.090
"and everything you're saying."

00:24:21.090 --> 00:24:22.803
It's like so encouraging.

00:24:23.830 --> 00:24:28.600
It's been really
rewarding, in that regard.

00:24:28.600 --> 00:24:31.210
I think of a lot of these
people that I work with as,

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:33.210
part of my extended family.

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:34.960
I look forward to seeing them everyday

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:38.680
and it's a wonderful place to be.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:39.513
I'm glad you asked me about that,

00:24:39.513 --> 00:24:41.423
because I don't always reflect on,

00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:43.920
on how great it is, actually.

00:24:43.920 --> 00:24:45.680
- So the media has been leveled.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:48.023
What do you think, Dana Perino?

00:24:48.023 --> 00:24:49.780
- (laughs) One of the
things about this time,

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:51.490
is I've realized that I can

00:24:51.490 --> 00:24:53.000
actually do everything from home.

00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:54.930
It's not ideal.

00:24:54.930 --> 00:24:56.920
Television and putting
on a television show,

00:24:56.920 --> 00:24:58.860
like The Daily Briefing or The Five.

00:24:58.860 --> 00:25:00.303
That is a team sport.

00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:03.600
Fox is probably the leanest company

00:25:03.600 --> 00:25:05.880
I've ever seen or been a part of.

00:25:05.880 --> 00:25:08.640
So, there's not a lot of spare capacity.

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:11.560
So, everybody trying to
do things from remote,

00:25:11.560 --> 00:25:14.240
it's difficult, but we have done it.

00:25:14.240 --> 00:25:16.160
There's never been a moment of dead air.

00:25:16.160 --> 00:25:18.300
We are on air all the time.

00:25:18.300 --> 00:25:20.770
But this morning I did two podcasts.

00:25:20.770 --> 00:25:22.880
One by Zoom, one by Skype.

00:25:22.880 --> 00:25:27.800
I have done my shows from here.

00:25:27.800 --> 00:25:28.633
What else did I do?

00:25:28.633 --> 00:25:30.810
Oh, I wrote an article for Foxnews.com,

00:25:30.810 --> 00:25:35.450
about how boredom can actually lead to

00:25:35.450 --> 00:25:36.863
great imaginations.

00:25:37.710 --> 00:25:38.543
I don't know if--
- Oh yeah!

00:25:38.543 --> 00:25:41.070
- When I was a kid, I
wasn't allowed to be bored.

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:43.483
My parents would say,
"Get outside and play.

00:25:44.564 --> 00:25:45.897
"You have no excuse
(laughing)

00:25:45.897 --> 00:25:46.887
"to be bored."

00:25:46.887 --> 00:25:49.090
If you look at like some
of these internet memes.

00:25:49.090 --> 00:25:49.923
They're hilarious.

00:25:49.923 --> 00:25:51.450
This guy Ian Prior.

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:53.720
He worked at the Justice
Department for awhile.

00:25:53.720 --> 00:25:56.900
He took the theme song of Growing Pains

00:25:56.900 --> 00:26:00.500
and made a video with his family,

00:26:00.500 --> 00:26:01.690
called, "Growing Bored."

00:26:01.690 --> 00:26:03.980
But it's to the same music
and with all the video.

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:08.110
It is so good and it's
amazing what people can do,

00:26:08.110 --> 00:26:09.283
just from home.

00:26:10.520 --> 00:26:11.745
Or anywhere they are.
- Did you know that

00:26:11.745 --> 00:26:12.610
Allen Thick wrote.

00:26:12.610 --> 00:26:14.610
Did you know that Alan
Thicke wrote that himself

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:17.690
and he also wrote the
theme song to Facts of Life

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:18.960
and a couple others?

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:20.330
- I had no idea.

00:26:20.330 --> 00:26:22.140
That's awesome, I love The Facts of Life.

00:26:22.140 --> 00:26:23.220
That was a great show.

00:26:23.220 --> 00:26:26.510
- Yeah, (laughs), it was a great show.

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:27.580
But what do you think about this,

00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:29.780
like just the generally
that now people can

00:26:29.780 --> 00:26:32.210
get their news from a
gajillion different things?

00:26:32.210 --> 00:26:36.302
You know we went from
decades of 6:30 p.m.,

00:26:36.302 --> 00:26:39.250
ABC, CBS, NBC.
- Oh I watched it all, yeah.

00:26:39.250 --> 00:26:41.810
- Yeah, but then we went from that.

00:26:41.810 --> 00:26:43.290
Then we had cable news.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:46.920
Now we have cable channels, proliferating.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:48.230
Then there's also this thing.

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:50.530
The podcast, YouTube, other world

00:26:50.530 --> 00:26:52.790
and now, everybody's
competing with everybody.

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:55.150
Like, do you find it hard to figure out

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:57.733
what is true through that whole mess?

00:26:59.160 --> 00:27:01.480
- Not really, because
I have trusted sources

00:27:01.480 --> 00:27:05.260
that I check and I'm a pretty
well rounded news consumer

00:27:05.260 --> 00:27:08.620
and I have a pretty strong
sense of what's real

00:27:08.620 --> 00:27:10.650
and what's not and I'm
also, as I mentioned before,

00:27:10.650 --> 00:27:12.490
I'm super cautious with my words

00:27:12.490 --> 00:27:15.290
and cautious with saying anything

00:27:15.290 --> 00:27:16.960
before I know for sure it's a go.

00:27:16.960 --> 00:27:20.130
One of my great fears for
The Daily Briefing is,

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:24.327
being one of the first
people in broadcast news to

00:27:24.327 --> 00:27:27.210
put out one of those deep fake videos,

00:27:27.210 --> 00:27:28.930
thinking that it's real.

00:27:28.930 --> 00:27:31.420
So I think it's a time where it's like

00:27:31.420 --> 00:27:33.773
crossing a river of crocodiles everyday.

00:27:35.860 --> 00:27:37.350
I don't know how it's gonna shake out,

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:41.650
but I also don't wanna be
somebody who longs for the past.

00:27:41.650 --> 00:27:45.490
I'm excited about all of
these great technologies.

00:27:45.490 --> 00:27:48.160
Fox I think has been really smart

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:50.200
in trying to figure out a way to

00:27:51.410 --> 00:27:53.850
deliver product for our consumers,

00:27:53.850 --> 00:27:56.670
that are still cable subscribers.

00:27:56.670 --> 00:27:59.030
But also, during this time of Coronavirus,

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:02.820
they took down the paywall
for the Fox News app.

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:05.050
So anybody that wants to watch Fox News,

00:28:05.050 --> 00:28:07.600
can do it on the app and
then I think they can

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:11.440
capture new viewers,
whether that be on our

00:28:11.440 --> 00:28:13.650
Facebook channel, or sorry,

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:15.680
the Facebook page or the YouTube channel.

00:28:15.680 --> 00:28:16.637
You know I started this thing called,

00:28:16.637 --> 00:28:20.160
"Storytime with Dana", right
when the Coronavirus started

00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:22.960
and everyday at 3:30
I read a book to kids.

00:28:22.960 --> 00:28:25.110
I just started doing it on Facebook Live,

00:28:25.110 --> 00:28:27.620
but Fox loved it so much that they took it

00:28:27.620 --> 00:28:29.740
and now they have it on Fox Nation,

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:30.930
which is a streaming service,

00:28:30.930 --> 00:28:33.310
FoxNews.com, the YouTube
channel, Facebook and Twitter,

00:28:33.310 --> 00:28:34.393
we get it all around.

00:28:35.310 --> 00:28:38.700
So, it's a lot of experimentation.

00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:40.650
It'll be fun to see where it all leads.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:44.550
- Yeah, so I know you're not a spokesman

00:28:44.550 --> 00:28:47.033
for the conservative movement, as a whole.

00:28:47.033 --> 00:28:47.866
(laughing)
But do you think

00:28:47.866 --> 00:28:51.140
conservatives maybe had
some branding issues,

00:28:51.140 --> 00:28:52.800
over the years that,
- Sure.

00:28:52.800 --> 00:28:53.710
- I think are actually,

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.650
I think actually are
melting away right now.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:57.257
I mean I think I'm
probably evidence of it,

00:28:57.257 --> 00:29:00.210
you know, when I go and
you guys are all nice to me

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:02.680
and I can talk and debate
and all that good stuff.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:03.753
But that for a long time,

00:29:03.753 --> 00:29:06.150
maybe just the word conservative,

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:08.270
it sounds kind of stogy and old

00:29:08.270 --> 00:29:09.730
and it's like why would a young person

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:11.629
wanna be conservative?

00:29:11.629 --> 00:29:15.240
That actually, some of that's
actually changing right now?

00:29:15.240 --> 00:29:16.180
- Absolutely.

00:29:16.180 --> 00:29:20.650
But everything cycles through the decades.

00:29:20.650 --> 00:29:22.360
You know National Review for me,

00:29:22.360 --> 00:29:25.503
my dad I think was a subscriber
since I was a little kid.

00:29:26.770 --> 00:29:30.340
I think he voted for Goldwater,
I believe that's right.

00:29:30.340 --> 00:29:32.933
So I just grew up in
that world, on a ranch.

00:29:33.810 --> 00:29:36.290
You know, individual
responsibility and state's rights,

00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:38.100
that was super important to all of us.

00:29:38.100 --> 00:29:39.780
But certainly things have changed.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:42.860
For example, I think
conservatives for a long time,

00:29:42.860 --> 00:29:44.690
were considered people who were against

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.710
the gay rights movement.

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:49.053
Who were against gay marriage.

00:29:50.510 --> 00:29:53.010
Over time, and not that long of a time,

00:29:53.010 --> 00:29:54.550
if you think about in history.

00:29:54.550 --> 00:29:55.743
That melted away.

00:29:56.883 --> 00:30:00.073
So that I think, allowed for people to

00:30:00.073 --> 00:30:02.010
give conservatism a different look,

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:04.883
but part of that is, what
are you comparing it to?

00:30:06.730 --> 00:30:10.310
If you are finding that
you are out of sorts with

00:30:10.310 --> 00:30:13.400
some of the nonsense on the left,

00:30:13.400 --> 00:30:15.520
then you might take another
look at conservatism

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:19.070
and you look at somebody
like Dan Crenshaw,

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:20.170
the Congressman from Texas.

00:30:20.170 --> 00:30:21.720
I did his podcast this morning.

00:30:22.900 --> 00:30:25.450
He's a younger, different
kind of conservative.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:27.040
Then you see him on Saturday Night Live,

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:30.900
responding with grace and
humor to Pete Davidson,

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:35.900
who had so casually attacked
him, mocking his war injury.

00:30:37.310 --> 00:30:41.300
So there's a lot of great
opportunity for conservatives.

00:30:41.300 --> 00:30:44.870
But, you know, it's a challenge,

00:30:44.870 --> 00:30:45.970
especially as you might imagine,

00:30:45.970 --> 00:30:47.720
there's all these different
ways to communicate now.

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:51.440
So how do you get to your ideas,

00:30:51.440 --> 00:30:53.900
to be persuasive for a
larger amount of people,

00:30:53.900 --> 00:30:55.220
especially younger people.

00:30:55.220 --> 00:30:56.053
That's a challenge.

00:30:56.053 --> 00:30:59.710
But I think it's going better than

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:01.523
some in the media might think it is.

00:31:03.040 --> 00:31:03.890
- Huh, interesting.

00:31:03.890 --> 00:31:04.990
All right, now I'm gonna ask you to put

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:07.020
the press secretary cap on, so.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:08.070
- Okay.

00:31:08.070 --> 00:31:09.640
- I'll give you two things here.

00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:12.120
One, if you were Trump's press secretary,

00:31:12.120 --> 00:31:14.350
would you want more responsibility

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:16.670
and want him to reign it in a little bit?

00:31:16.670 --> 00:31:20.590
And two, if you were
Biden's press secretary,

00:31:20.590 --> 00:31:22.955
would you want him doing
these live streams,

00:31:22.955 --> 00:31:24.000
(laughing)
that are in my

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:27.113
humble opinion, coming
off pretty terribly?

00:31:27.113 --> 00:31:29.210
- I think if I were
Trump's press secretary,

00:31:29.210 --> 00:31:31.320
the one thing I would not wanna be,

00:31:31.320 --> 00:31:34.983
is blindsided and surprised.

00:31:36.200 --> 00:31:37.560
That's why I would never
be Trump's (laughs)

00:31:37.560 --> 00:31:38.630
press secretary.

00:31:38.630 --> 00:31:39.870
But I already had that job.
(laughing)

00:31:39.870 --> 00:31:41.380
They're never gonna ask me, but,

00:31:41.380 --> 00:31:43.560
you know, I think that
it's incredibly difficult

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:47.940
to think about knowing
an answer at 11 o'clock,

00:31:47.940 --> 00:31:49.540
giving that answer at 12 o'clock

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:53.380
and having it undermined at 12:06 p.m.

00:31:53.380 --> 00:31:55.760
But that is how he operates.

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:58.410
Some people love it, some
people think it's horrible.

00:31:59.310 --> 00:32:01.923
The Biden question is a
little bit more difficult.

00:32:02.830 --> 00:32:04.550
I don't know what I would do.

00:32:04.550 --> 00:32:06.140
They're in a real fix right now,

00:32:06.140 --> 00:32:09.340
because, Joe Biden is now
the presumptive nominee.

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:10.930
He's gotten no bang for that buck,

00:32:10.930 --> 00:32:12.760
after Bernie Sanders dropped out.

00:32:12.760 --> 00:32:14.640
Everybody's sort of like, "Yeah, okay."

00:32:14.640 --> 00:32:16.890
Then Biden says, "I'm
gonna start my VP search."

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:19.207
And we're like, "Oh okay,
well there's about 10 women

00:32:19.207 --> 00:32:21.190
"that you might consider, so oh well."

00:32:21.190 --> 00:32:24.350
I mean people are just so zeroed in on,

00:32:24.350 --> 00:32:26.940
appropriately so, the health of the nation

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:29.210
and the economic health of the nation.

00:32:29.210 --> 00:32:31.110
I think that if Biden were to come out

00:32:31.110 --> 00:32:33.700
and push too hard against
President Trump right now

00:32:33.700 --> 00:32:36.330
and criticize, the Commander
in Chief in the time of

00:32:36.330 --> 00:32:41.330
a major, not just a domestic
crisis, a global crisis,

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:44.410
it would look like he
was rooting for failure

00:32:44.410 --> 00:32:45.740
for the country.

00:32:45.740 --> 00:32:48.883
So he's gotta pick his spots
very carefully and sharply.

00:32:50.430 --> 00:32:53.300
I just don't know if he's
gonna be able to do that.

00:32:53.300 --> 00:32:56.250
- Do you sense though
that some in the media,

00:32:56.250 --> 00:32:59.570
are rooting for the
collapse of the economy

00:32:59.570 --> 00:33:01.317
and just that they view this as,

00:33:01.317 --> 00:33:03.727
"Oh we didn't get Trump on Russia.

00:33:03.727 --> 00:33:07.127
"We didn't get Trump
on Ukraine impeachment,

00:33:07.127 --> 00:33:08.609
"blah, blah, blah."
- I don't think so.

00:33:08.609 --> 00:33:09.442
No.
- That this,

00:33:09.442 --> 00:33:11.253
you don't think so, huh?

00:33:12.090 --> 00:33:14.724
- No, I don't.

00:33:14.724 --> 00:33:18.180
I don't, I mean I consume a ton of news.

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:19.870
I'm not naive to the biases,

00:33:19.870 --> 00:33:22.350
but, there's a lot of problems out there.

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:24.550
I mean, like the Food Lions.

00:33:24.550 --> 00:33:27.430
It's just the video, you
can see from overhead,

00:33:27.430 --> 00:33:31.583
a three mile long car
line, to get food in Miami.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:36.130
A six mile long car line
to get food in San Antonio.

00:33:36.130 --> 00:33:37.850
I mean those are just facts.

00:33:37.850 --> 00:33:38.683
Those are happening.

00:33:38.683 --> 00:33:41.370
I don't think that they
root for the failure.

00:33:41.370 --> 00:33:43.730
They might root for
President Trump to lose.

00:33:43.730 --> 00:33:45.552
But I don't think that
they root for failure,

00:33:45.552 --> 00:33:48.103
for people, I really don't.

00:33:49.540 --> 00:33:50.780
- Yeah, I hope you're right.

00:33:50.780 --> 00:33:53.410
I mean I see some people that
are pretty mainstream voices,

00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.910
that it's almost like
they just want this thing

00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:57.846
to continue, you know,

00:33:57.846 --> 00:33:58.728
maybe--
- Well I mean that

00:33:58.728 --> 00:34:00.900
was true like during the
financial crisis of 2008, right?

00:34:00.900 --> 00:34:02.580
They were like, "Oh now Bush."

00:34:02.580 --> 00:34:03.913
But it was like, well,

00:34:05.980 --> 00:34:08.540
if you didn't shore up the banks

00:34:08.540 --> 00:34:09.780
and you ended up with something worse

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:10.980
than the Great Depression,

00:34:10.980 --> 00:34:12.580
what would've been the better choice?

00:34:12.580 --> 00:34:15.040
Again, presidents can't worry about that.

00:34:15.040 --> 00:34:18.410
President Trump has to,
again, lift his gaze

00:34:18.410 --> 00:34:21.350
and not worry about the
headline of the day.

00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:23.210
Lift his gaze, look out.

00:34:23.210 --> 00:34:25.810
People are gonna judge this
for years and years and years.

00:34:25.810 --> 00:34:27.010
Forget about what they're gonna say

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:28.280
within the next 24 hours.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:30.148
Are you making the best decisions,

00:34:30.148 --> 00:34:32.500
with the information you have at the time,

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:35.460
on behalf of the nation and
can you defend that decision?

00:34:35.460 --> 00:34:37.210
Then that's the decision you should make,

00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:38.790
you should defend it as much as you can

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:40.790
and promote it, whatever you need to do.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:42.967
But I wouldn't get so mired in,

00:34:42.967 --> 00:34:44.060
"Oh, they're against me."

00:34:44.060 --> 00:34:46.760
Because, I think self pity in politics

00:34:46.760 --> 00:34:49.283
is pretty pathetic and pitiful.

00:34:49.283 --> 00:34:52.670
You just need to win, you
just have to try harder

00:34:52.670 --> 00:34:53.503
to win the arguments.

00:34:53.503 --> 00:34:54.680
It just takes a lot more effort.

00:34:54.680 --> 00:34:56.467
That's why I always tell companies,

00:34:56.467 --> 00:34:58.587
"If you think you're
gonna have some problems,

00:34:58.587 --> 00:35:00.067
"always hire somebody that's worked in

00:35:00.067 --> 00:35:02.055
"Republican politics or communications,

00:35:02.055 --> 00:35:03.367
(laughing)
"because, guaranteed,

00:35:03.367 --> 00:35:04.817
"they've had to work harder."

00:35:06.340 --> 00:35:08.865
- They've been through it once or twice.

00:35:08.865 --> 00:35:09.750
- Yeah.

00:35:09.750 --> 00:35:12.200
- Yeah, do you think that Trump though,

00:35:12.200 --> 00:35:13.720
sort of needs that in a way?

00:35:13.720 --> 00:35:16.100
Like it's almost like him and Jim Acosta,

00:35:16.100 --> 00:35:18.520
are just like a match made
in Hell, sort of thing.

00:35:18.520 --> 00:35:20.750
Like, CNN needs it for eyeballs

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:22.660
and Trump needs it, because that's like,

00:35:22.660 --> 00:35:24.500
sort of what gives him juice.

00:35:24.500 --> 00:35:25.596
So they're playing

00:35:25.596 --> 00:35:26.472
this game
- It's a symbiotic

00:35:26.472 --> 00:35:27.567
relationship,
- but if anything.

00:35:27.567 --> 00:35:29.840
Yeah, yeah.
- Yep the symbiotic

00:35:29.840 --> 00:35:32.250
relationship, they feed off of each other.

00:35:32.250 --> 00:35:35.040
But I was watching a
press conference recently

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:37.797
and I heard myself saying,
"That's a ridiculous question.

00:35:37.797 --> 00:35:39.137
"That's a dumb question."

00:35:40.270 --> 00:35:42.470
But, look, our founding fathers were

00:35:42.470 --> 00:35:45.290
really, really brilliant
and the First Amendment

00:35:45.290 --> 00:35:47.360
was first for a reason.

00:35:47.360 --> 00:35:48.830
Because it's most important.

00:35:48.830 --> 00:35:50.930
So we have to safe guard
it and if they wanna

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:54.073
ask stupid questions, that's, so be it.

00:35:55.480 --> 00:35:56.313
- All right, I got two more for you

00:35:56.313 --> 00:35:58.420
